    "answer_en": "My background matches this role because I have spent more than 18 years across software engineering, enterprise applications, architecture, digital transformation and operational support, and a large part of that experience is in a government healthcare environment where technology directly supports procurement, supply, inventory and management decisions. At the Egyptian Unified Procurement Authority, my role has gone beyond software delivery. I have been responsible for evolving business-critical systems, translating operational requirements into application and integration changes, coordinating technical teams and vendors, managing access and support concerns, and ensuring that reporting and data are useful to decision-makers. One concrete use case is healthcare supply and inventory management: procurement, receiving, stock, supplier information and reporting cannot be treated as separate islands. They depend on consistent master data, controlled roles, reliable integrations, user adoption and operational support. I have worked on those problems directly, including dashboards and reporting, product/entity mapping, business-to-IT coordination, training/support and integration between operational and analytical or financial processes. That is why I see a strong fit with the WaCA Systems Manager role. The MSF products and humanitarian context are different, and I would not pretend otherwise, but the management problem is familiar: own the application portfolio as services, keep them available and secure, manage lifecycle and vendors, maintain data quality and integration, support users and make sure technology enables the operation rather than becoming a bottleneck. My strongest value is that I can work on both sides of that equation—technical architecture and operational business reality.",
