    "answer_en": "On-Demand provides flexibility without long-term commitment and is useful for uncertain or variable workloads. Savings Plans exchange a usage commitment for lower compute prices and are generally flexible across eligible compute usage depending on the plan. Reserved Instances provide pricing benefits and, for some types, capacity-related characteristics tied to specific services/configurations. Spot uses spare capacity at deep discounts but can be interrupted, so it suits fault-tolerant or flexible workloads rather than irreplaceable single-instance production. I would baseline usage first, keep resilience intact, then commit only the stable portion of demand and use Spot for interruption-tolerant work.",
