    "answer_en": "I use risk-based patching with asset and vulnerability context. Systems are grouped by criticality and exposure; routine patch rings move from test/pilot to broader production; critical exploited vulnerabilities have an emergency path with accelerated approval and compensating controls if immediate patching is unsafe. Pre-checks, backup/rollback, maintenance communication and post-validation are standard. Unsupported/EOL systems are tracked separately because 'fully patched' can still mean insecure. Endpoint/server compliance dashboards should show age of critical patches and exceptions, while vulnerability management includes configuration weaknesses and third-party software, not only OS updates. For remote sites, I plan bandwidth and local caching/staging so patching does not compete with medical or operational traffic.",
