    "answer_en": "I choose based on data model and access pattern, not on a generic claim that NoSQL is faster. RDS/Aurora fit relational transactions, joins, mature SQL ecosystems and applications that benefit from managed relational engines. Aurora adds an AWS-optimized relational architecture and scaling/availability characteristics. DynamoDB fits key-value/document workloads with known access patterns, very high scale and serverless operational characteristics. I would also consider team skills, reporting needs, offline synchronization model, data consistency requirements, portability and cost. For a humanitarian system, supportability and data correctness can matter more than theoretical peak throughput.",
