    "answer_en": "For information systems, 'Do No Harm' means that a technically useful solution can still be unacceptable if it increases risk to patients, communities or staff. I would apply data minimization, purpose limitation, strong access control, encryption, retention and secure deletion, but I would also ask contextual questions: who could gain access, what harm could identification create, is the data really needed, and what happens if a device or site is seized? MSF's Health Records Management Policy explicitly places patient interests, do-no-harm, rights and health ethics at the centre when principles conflict. So my design reviews would include not only cybersecurity and compliance, but also humanitarian consequence and safe fallback procedures.",
