    "question_en": "How do you approach protecting patient/beneficiary data in line with international humanitarian data protection standards?",
    "answer_en": "I would start from the fact that health and beneficiary data can create real-world harm if exposed. The control model is therefore lifecycle-based: map what data exists and why, minimize collection, classify sensitivity, define legal and operational retention, encrypt data in transit and at rest, enforce least-privilege access, maintain auditability, and securely transfer or delete records when their purpose ends. For especially sensitive contexts I would also consider the risk of device seizure, compelled disclosure and re-identification of supposedly aggregated data. MSF's own Health Records Management Policy makes security, minimization, quality, accessibility, retention and accountability explicit. IT should work with medical, data-protection and legal owners rather than treating this as a purely technical problem.",
