    "question_en": "What is your experience with cloud migration in connectivity-constrained environments?",
    "answer_en": "My approach to cloud migration is workload-led and connectivity-aware. I classify each application's dependencies, data sensitivity, latency tolerance, offline behavior, integration pattern, RTO/RPO, user locations and support skills before choosing rehost, replatform, replace, retain or retire. For weak-connectivity missions I minimize chatty protocols and large transfers, use local caching or offline capture where the business process supports it, schedule bulk synchronization, and test from realistic field links—not only HQ. I also plan identity, logging, backup, rollback and operating cost before cutover. My direct strength is Azure/enterprise-systems governance; where AWS is involved, I would apply the same controls while working within AWS-native architecture and service guidance.",
